1
00:00:00,180 --> 00:00:05,680
Imagine betting like millions of taxpayer dollars on a massive organizational software system,

2
00:00:06,520 --> 00:00:09,980
only to realize later that, um, nobody actually checked if it works.

3
00:00:10,600 --> 00:00:16,239
Yeah. That is how you end up with work that's completely fragmented and, you know, locked into one supplier.

4
00:00:16,640 --> 00:00:25,060
Exactly. Welcome to the Deep Dive. Today, we're unpacking the rigorous methodology of how these massive government systems are audited and verified.

5
00:00:25,680 --> 00:00:29,920
Because whether you're evaluating a tech stack or managing a sprawling project,

6
00:00:30,560 --> 00:00:34,460
a questionnaire answer is, well, it's just what a body says about itself.

7
00:00:34,820 --> 00:00:39,000
Right. It's a claim. And a desk result is just what the public record suggests.

8
00:00:39,060 --> 00:00:45,980
Yeah. Neither of those is a verified finding yet, and honestly, they can be easily overturned by the first official who disagrees.

9
00:00:46,560 --> 00:00:53,940
So understanding how to turn those loose claims into bulletproof findings is essential if you want to avoid siloed or duplicated work.

10
00:00:54,400 --> 00:00:58,620
It really is. And that transformation from a claim to a verified finding,

11
00:00:59,260 --> 00:01:02,100
it requires a highly structured sequence of activities.

12
00:01:02,440 --> 00:01:04,879
It's not just, you know, double-checking a spreadsheet.

13
00:01:05,360 --> 00:01:07,300
So what does that sequence actually look like?

14
00:01:07,660 --> 00:01:10,260
Well, there are six specific activities in order.

15
00:01:10,780 --> 00:01:12,720
First, you receive the signed questionnaires.

16
00:01:13,220 --> 00:01:17,420
Then two, you review every single document they name against the desk results.

17
00:01:18,080 --> 00:01:20,960
Okay, so you're basically doing a side-by-side comparison.

18
00:01:21,540 --> 00:01:26,180
Exactly. Then step three is clarification interviews on each disputed point.

19
00:01:26,760 --> 00:01:32,680
After that comes four, the technical workshops where you actually see the systems working, like on a screen or a log.

20
00:01:32,940 --> 00:01:34,980
Oh, wow. So you're right in there.

21
00:01:35,560 --> 00:01:39,840
Yeah. Then five is synthesis, which means writing a verified position for every point.

22
00:01:40,380 --> 00:01:45,840
And finally, six, you hold a final validation workshop with all the bodies to close out any disputed points.

23
00:01:46,500 --> 00:01:47,200
Okay, but,

24
00:01:47,940 --> 00:01:48,880
and this is a big but,

25
00:01:49,600 --> 00:01:53,500
when sources or officials inevitably disagree during this process,

26
00:01:54,320 --> 00:01:58,280
how do you settle the debate, like without it just becoming a battle of opinions?

27
00:01:59,060 --> 00:02:00,900
That is where the evidence ladder comes in.

28
00:02:01,240 --> 00:02:08,000
It's a strict rule. When sources disagree, the higher level always wins, and, um, there are five rungs to this ladder.

29
00:02:08,520 --> 00:02:09,620
Okay. Walk me through them.

30
00:02:10,020 --> 00:02:10,080
Yep.

31
00:02:10,740 --> 00:02:13,860
So at the bottom, rung five is a statement in an interview.

32
00:02:14,160 --> 00:02:16,380
Above that, rung four is an internal report.

33
00:02:16,860 --> 00:02:19,080
Rung three is official statistics.

34
00:02:19,360 --> 00:02:20,260
Okay. Makes sense.

35
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:21,560
And the top two?

36
00:02:22,460 --> 00:02:27,020
Rung two is an official document in force, like a law or an approved budget.

37
00:02:27,460 --> 00:02:31,880
And the ultimate authority, rung one, is a system actually seen working.

38
00:02:32,320 --> 00:02:35,900
Wait, so an interview is the absolute weakest form of evidence?

39
00:02:36,380 --> 00:02:40,660
Yeah, it is. Because to reach the verification standard, you need either

40
00:02:41,300 --> 00:02:47,740
one source at level one, the system seen working, or two independent sources at levels two or three.

41
00:02:48,160 --> 00:02:51,340
So if you just have two officials contradicting each other in interviews?

42
00:02:52,020 --> 00:02:54,040
Then that point is still disputed, exactly.

43
00:02:54,640 --> 00:02:58,860
And the reference architecture actually names four specific templates to carry this work.

44
00:02:59,300 --> 00:03:07,300
You've got the map of bodies involved, the session plan, the reconciled response table, and the final validation workshop agenda.

45
00:03:07,880 --> 00:03:10,780
Okay, I think I get the framework, but let's see this in action.

46
00:03:11,360 --> 00:03:12,100
How does this play out,

47
00:03:12,760 --> 00:03:13,800
you know, in a practical setting?

48
00:03:14,300 --> 00:03:21,020
Let's look at Progressa. And just to be clear, Progressa is a fictional country, um, used for these exact models.

49
00:03:21,380 --> 00:03:26,620
But during their audit, the team was evaluating the ministry's school information system called PEMIS.

50
00:03:26,860 --> 00:03:33,420
Right. So a desk review suggested that PEMIS held learner level data, which means individual student records.

51
00:03:33,460 --> 00:03:35,440
Which obviously would be super useful to have.

52
00:03:35,600 --> 00:03:42,180
Definitely. But during session V dash zero one, a technical workshop, the team asked to see the system working.

53
00:03:42,320 --> 00:03:43,500
That's level one evidence.

54
00:03:44,140 --> 00:03:49,260
And, uh, they found out it only held totals by district, grade, and sex.

55
00:03:49,520 --> 00:03:52,920
Oh, wow. So it didn't have individual student data at all?

56
00:03:53,060 --> 00:03:53,400
Not at all.

57
00:03:54,280 --> 00:03:58,420
And because level one evidence overrides a desk review, the point was instantly corrected.

58
00:03:58,980 --> 00:04:04,440
It's like reading a car's sales brochure versus, you know, actually looking under the hood to see the engine.

59
00:04:05,060 --> 00:04:08,180
The brochure is the desk review, but seeing the engine run is level one.

60
00:04:08,280 --> 00:04:14,540
I mean, exactly. It saves you from making decisions based on data that is totally siloed or costly to change down the line.

